<p>The UK government said its position on the Falkland Islands remains unchanged after reports of a leaked internal Pentagon email suggesting the United States could reconsider its diplomatic backing for British sovereignty over the territory. Downing Street said sovereignty rests with the UK and stressed the islanders’ right to self-determination, while reports said the Pentagon review was discussed as part of possible retaliation after Britain did not join US action in the Iran war. The episode adds strain to an important alliance just before King Charles III is due to make a three-day state visit to the United States, but it has also prompted a notably united response in Britain, with government and opposition figures backing the same core position on sovereignty. For now, the report points to a diplomatic dispute rather than any immediate change on the ground in the South Atlantic, and the cited coverage describes the Pentagon document as an internal proposal or review rather than a final US policy decision.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/FiguVq3bepnN3BPA_YCQldFW2soRjFMSv7nroczPDtLooV_QKq9J6UW37AUthuvLvo23o-5MRoz5-CWbiw6pCjdfC5kMWUwBpUXRdP6dL_onlI6qL7aydTR8xQxu50_ZuAJ0kmEjGhcSMUNGREtnFBYPysLZIvYwhkHB1Y_-85Qb4YH1CxZtO8IUDXbvdPYcX1gBM_wxwxCcviann0EQcYE1E-5fGzTWUIHGZxvYSwniX9ylJnMw7ptG6gDNDd0HQ6ubioZBKzQb6KR1OLIqOU0' alt='Image used with coverage of the UK response to the leaked Pentagon memo on the Falklands.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Cross-party unity: British political parties and the Falkland Islands government closed ranks after the leak, with both government and opposition figures saying sovereignty is not open to negotiation.</li><li>State visit timing: The disclosure comes just before King Charles III’s planned three-day state visit to the United States, raising the diplomatic stakes in how both governments handle the issue publicly.</li><li>Internal memo: Reuters, cited by MercoPress, said the leaked memorandum was part of a broader package of retaliatory options aimed at NATO allies that did not back US military action against Iran.</li><li>Self-determination: A UK official quoted by the Financial Times defended the Falkland Islanders’ right to self-determination, reinforcing London’s legal and political case for maintaining sovereignty.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>UK government: Downing Street said sovereignty over the Falkland Islands rests with the UK and that London’s position will not change despite the reported Pentagon review. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cde51y0zgjyo'>BBC News</a>)</li><li>British political parties: Government and opposition politicians responded in unusually unified terms, saying the Falklands issue is not open for debate after the leak. (<a href='https://en.mercopress.com/2026/04/25/uk-political-reaction-to-pentagon-leak-falklands-sovereignty-is-not-up-for-debate'>MercoPress</a>)</li><li>US defense officials: The leaked Pentagon email reportedly explored whether Washington should reconsider diplomatic support for Britain over the islands as part of retaliation tied to the UK’s stance on Iran. (<a href='https://en.mercopress.com/2026/04/24/us-weighs-withdrawing-diplomatic-support-for-uk-over-falklands'>MercoPress</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2026/apr/24/uk-position-falklands-unchanged-leaked-pentagon-memo'>UK position on Falklands will not change, No 10 says after leaked Pentagon memo</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://en.mercopress.com/2026/04/25/uk-political-reaction-to-pentagon-leak-falklands-sovereignty-is-not-up-for-debate'>UK political reaction to Pentagon leak: &#x27;Falklands sovereignty is not up for debate&#x27;</a> - mercopress.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/b2d76a38-7014-4a99-940a-f02cf4c191a2'>Sovereignty of Falklands ‘not in question’, says Number 10 after leaked US memo report</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cde51y0zgjyo'>No 10 says Falklands sovereignty rests with UK after report of US &#x27;review&#x27;</a> - bbc.com</li></ul>